WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.227
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.420
SPEAKER: Enterprises
have long relied on KPIs

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.770
to align all areas
of the business,

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.550
and this applies to digital
initiatives as well.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:14.590
Many executives at
large enterprises

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.170
are now connecting the dots
between the performance

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.300
of their core business
and their relevance

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.900
of digital capabilities
powered by APIs.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.600
To facilitate the next
steps in realizing value

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.090
from these critical
assets, enterprises

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:32.229
need to become savvy in the use
of key performance indicators,

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:35.470
or KPIs, to drive
digital programs.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:38.890
Good KPIs are sometimes not
obvious at first glance,

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.040
and supporting metrics
play an important role.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.940
Stronger programs will often
proactively shape their highest

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.280
level goals and
KPIs to anticipate

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.720
broader transformation.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.370
Following some
important guidelines.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:56.260
Number 1, focus on driving
growth, breadth, and speed

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.900
of API adoption by the
application programs that

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.430
depend on them.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.150
2, accelerate the
velocity of iterations

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.760
in, not only API
and app development,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.540
but also the creation of user
facing digital experiences.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.020
And 3, align the metrics
of the API program

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.860
with developers and channel
partners using those APIs.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:20.740
We have seen some common
pitfalls when defining metrics

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.720
at the onset of API programs.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.530
The program should not be
a standalone P/L function

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.750
with metrics set in isolation.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.750
Generally, avoid
using number of APIs

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:33.970
produce as a top line target.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:37.180
Publishing API is not the
same as creating value

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.460
through the consumption
of that API.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.480
Lastly, avoid measuring your
API program like a project.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.940
Targeting and making
decisions on APIs

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.970
in traditional terms of
budget, timeline, and quality

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:52.810
will force managers to focus
on completion and timeline,

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.790
rather than creating value.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:56.740
The traditional project
metrics can still

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.600
be measured but
always be careful

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.830
of incentives and behaviors
this will cast on your team.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:05.230
Whatever direction you
take, the first step

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:07.990
is to establish KPIs
for your API program

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:11.260
that are direct contributors
to enterprise metrics.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.290
Connect these enterprise
metrics to digital experiences,

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.750
and measure the velocity at
which digital experiences are

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:17.740
created.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.960
These are most
important metrics.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:23.590
In most cases, the API programs
to succeed the enterprise

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.710
must align metrics for all
elements of the digital value

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.520
chain, including backend
systems, API teams, API

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.340
developers, apps, and end users.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.780
This might be a lot to
process at one time,

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:38.140
so let's take a
look, one at a time,

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.740
at some possible KPIs
in your API program.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.300
We'll describe what
each one of them

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:47.440
are, their benefits, and some of
the pitfalls and potential sub

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.400
metrics.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.450
One common metric we
see is number of APIs.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:55.030
Often used to drive
short-term productivity,

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.010
this target often
ignores the fact

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:00.310
that APIs must also
have easy use utility.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.580
Segment out your APIs by
their various functions

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.560
or capabilities
and look for ways

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.890
to measure reusability
rather than just volume.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.810
Tracking the number
of developers

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:14.410
is a target commonly
intended to improve adoption.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:17.650
You can target metrics
developers retention rates,

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.540
as well as the number
of active developers

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.490
within your community.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:24.340
Some also use a
conversion funnel approach

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.180
to see how they can convert from
developers who are browsing,

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.150
registered but not
active, to trial user,

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.430
to actual production usage.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.760
Be cautious in making this
a singular focus, though.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:39.340
Pair this with feedback
from developers

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.580
and look at where developers
drop off over time.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:45.490
Customers don't
experience APIs directly.

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:49.970
They only experience the apps
that they use the APIs with.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:51.860
Be careful using this KPI.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:54.890
This target is important,
so measure this

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:58.130
with good API instrumentation
and app analytics.

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:00.560
For example, knowing
how many customers

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.140
are connected to all
of your company's apps

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.210
using the same
authentication API

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:10.220
would be a great way to showcase
the re-use of that API itself.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:11.930
Depending on the
type of business

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:15.650
you have, this may or may
not be an immediate KPI.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.459
However, the potential of
APIs to expand your ecosystem

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:21.980
is a powerful capability.

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.830
Use this target to
reach out to partners,

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.530
drive adoption, and
demonstrate the success

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:29.250
of the platform expansion.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.210
This is a key ecosystem
metric for any platform.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.410
This target may be
useful to drive reach.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:40.340
Use this target by segmenting
the apps of partner

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:43.400
and/or internal and
by line of business.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.190
If the program creates a lot
of apps that are only used

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:48.680
internally and not
by customers, it

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:51.830
can sometimes feed internal
criticism and abandonment

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:53.240
of the program.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.670
This one is a great
productivity metric.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:59.390
In order to enable app teams
to rapidly create new customer

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:02.990
experiences, API teams should
become adept at quickly

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.360
turning around needed APIs.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.960
Some teams will create a metric
called time to hello world

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:12.080
describing and benchmarking
the end to end time

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.660
to create a very basic API.

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.750
You can define it any way
you want, but it's a catchy

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.240
and instantly
understood concept.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.960
Reducing developer friction
to access your API platform

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.550
is a key target.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.280
Start measuring this
early on and distinguish

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:31.070
between the duration
for the initial signup,

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:33.440
the duration it takes
to get apps approved,

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:36.670
to the duration it takes
to go live with an app.

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:40.240
Traffic growth helps with
both indication of usage

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.370
but also scalability.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:46.570
This target helps API programs
develop a strong DevOps

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:49.600
and RunOps culture by
continuously monitoring,

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:53.170
improving, and driving
value through the APIs.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:56.500
Be sure to target this
with a related metrics

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:59.950
up and down the value chain,
including latency, reliability,

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:01.600
and scalability.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.420
This target focuses
the API program

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:06.730
to build relevance
across the business

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:10.300
and to drive re-use of the APIs
through backend assets they

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.470
connect to.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:15.010
Particularly valuable APIs, such
as those that provide access

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:18.040
to unique data or
proprietary functionality,

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.440
may become a greater
asset to partners

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:23.290
and provide entrance
to new markets.

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:26.500
This KPI also helps the
team escalate blockers

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:30.040
to the proper executive
level for resolution.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:33.070
APIs themselves don't
always reduce costs,

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:36.610
but they are critical to broader
internal refactoring of value

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:39.462
chains, which do reduce costs.

00:06:39.462 --> 00:06:40.920
It can be hard to
initially measure

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:43.710
cost reduction from
APIs, and sometimes it

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:46.140
stirs dissent amongst managers.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:50.730
Significant cost reductions are
usually realized from the rise

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.340
in API reuse and
increase in APIs,

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.320
allowing the company
to purchase more

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.450
of the off-the-shelf software.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:02.490
For example, HR can replace old
internal systems and process

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:06.240
by exposing the data through
APIs to better Cloud-based HR

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.460
software, even more compelling.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.350
Instead of taking
capital investment

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:13.110
and building new consumer
facing or customer facing

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:15.720
applications, the
company can invite

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:19.050
partners to co-innovate and
build multiple experiences,

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:22.470
leveraging the company's
APIs and core assets.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:25.830
This leads to a reduction
from reduced research

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:29.370
and development spend within
the company and in relation

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.590
to the speed and
market, the new channels

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:35.220
the company is able to access
by going to multiple markets

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:36.570
with multiple partners.

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:40.290
Direct revenue can be
a tricky KPI to target.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:41.790
APIs are enablers.

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:44.670
They need an adoption cycle
before proving their relevance

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:48.382
to revenue, also it's a
purely API Business model.

00:07:48.382 --> 00:07:50.340
If you're not one of
these kinds of businesses,

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.650
you might see a way
to upcharge and claim

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.200
a share of the revenue
for the usage of your APIs

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.220
similar to cost of goods
sold or chargeback method.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.510
Instead of targeting NPS of
those digital experiences,

00:08:02.510 --> 00:08:04.950
use NPS to gauge the
app developers who

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:08.160
are adopting and calling
APIs from within their apps.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.170
To get even more
detailed, you can

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:12.000
measure the NPS
between internal app

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.900
developers versus external
and partner app developers.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.750
In early stages, your
APIs and documentation

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:20.490
may be really immature.

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:24.300
So NPS is less useful than
just getting qualitative user

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:25.870
feedback.

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:29.010
Another aspect to target
is the quality of APIs.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:32.280
Track response times,
error rates on your APIs,

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:36.299
and whether they meet the SLA
and standards you're expecting.

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:40.409
Don't boil the ocean and focus
your team on a handful or less

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:42.539
of measurements that are
important to the program's

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:43.650
success.

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:47.190
You can prioritize them
or change them over time.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:50.100
KPIs are metrics
are living tools

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:53.100
and should be
revisited periodically.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.960
What is important
at the beginning

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:57.400
will not be important later on.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.130
Here's an example how we
suggest pacing these metrics

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:02.700
and their focus areas over time.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:04.830
When getting started
in the crawl phase,

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:08.250
track the number and type of
APIs producing the engagement

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:09.900
of the app developers.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:14.400
Focus on execution improvement
with KPIs for speed and quality

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.200
of API development.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:19.230
Once APIs are in
use, shift the focus

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:22.440
to adoption and increase
of the ecosystem.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.542
Focus on the number of apps,
the number of partners,

00:09:25.542 --> 00:09:27.000
or the number of
customers that are

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.670
using the newly minted APIs.

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:31.920
Once a good production
cadence around APIs

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.190
is operational
and begins to run,

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.290
add more familiar
business impact

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:41.130
reporting to the dashboard,
such as direct revenue, NPS,

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:44.550
breadth of business, and
cost reduction as agreed to

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.800
with the rest of
the executive team.

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:48.930
If you want more
information, please check out

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:51.480
the community for
ebooks, articles,

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:53.282
and other related topics.

00:09:53.282 --> 00:09:54.240
Thank you for watching.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

